A||N||18||FieroSoul||84 engine swap questions||||6||
Z||000000||FieroSoul||08-29-2001||06:36 AM|||| I have been told that swapping engines in the 84 is more difficult because of wiring problems or a need to rewire the front to get things to work. Does anyone have specific information on this.  Just what needs to be switched and why?  THanks,||||reg||6
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-29-2001||07:59 AM||||If you swap  with an 84 engine, no problem. The wires front of the firewall for an 84 were set up with the 4 cylinder engine. No V6 option available.  The 85-88 4 cylinder cars had all wires for the V6 in front of the firewall, so thats plug and play.  So when you swap a V6 into an 84 body, the wires don't match up for a V6 to the 84 harness.  You will need to meld the two wire harnesses together if you want all the components to work harmoneously.<P>You would need the wiring diagrams of the engine and the wiring diagram for the 84.  I don't believe it is rocket science to do, maybe very detailed and tedious to do.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||08-29-2001||08:11 AM||||Also I believe you'll need to change your fuel delivery system (pump, etc) to that of a v-6 car.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSoul||08-30-2001||06:39 AM|||| Okay, that makes sense and I think I can handle that. What about the lights? Maybe that is what the person was trying to tell me. If I swap ECMs to match the wiring with the engine then my headlights won't work unless I switch that system too, right?  And yes, it also makes sense that my fuel system would be too weak. Any more advice?||||reg||6
